 "campaign_id": "qwen35-9b-w4a16-tp2-rowwise-r0/r1 (2026-09-07)",
 "question": "Whether the two-card identity loss comes from the cross-card reduction. On one card this route is exact at every rung through 64 users in both passes; on two cards it scores 63/64 at 64 users, and the 4B shows the same shape. XCCL's reduction order depends on message size, so an [M,N] all-reduce need not agree with M separate [1,N] all-reduces, which would make a step depend on how many rows share it.",
 "method": "A diagnostic overlay image, R276 plus a row-wise TP all-reduce (pure Python; every pinned kernel digest unchanged, the replaced communicator declared through EXPECTED_XPU_COMMUNICATOR_SHA256). Two arms on that same image so the rebuild is not the variable: r0 with the row-wise path off, r1 with it on for inputs of 2..64 rows.",
 "image": {
  "tag": "neural-download/vllm-openai-xpu:qwen38-int4-r276-rowwise-allreduce-diag",
  "id": "sha256:30226665158aedffc34806565bf362eee1abb407350edf5303d21c46ba59ee46",
  "xpu_communicator_sha256": "97c75747e93e557dae3db632f3aab2cc38649eddb561316737cb596816c9f2fa"
 },
 "control_validity": "r0 reproduces the published R276 result: 63/64 at 64 users in both passes at 2091.9/2093.0 tok/s against the R276 control's 63/64 twice at 2083.7/2092.9. The overlay changes nothing on its own.",
 "finding": "Not a fix, and not enough evidence to call it a partial one. With the row-wise all-reduce on, 64 users without speculation scored 64/64 in the first pass and 63/64 in the second, against a control that scored 63/64 in both passes on two separate images. That is a one-request difference in one pass of two, which supports no claim by itself. What it does establish is that the cross-card all-reduce is not by itself the whole cause: forcing every 2..64-row reduction onto the same path the single-row step uses did not reliably restore identity.",
 "cost": "Without speculation the row-wise path is close to free at the rung where it matters: 2086.7/2088.2 tok/s against 2091.9/2093.0, about -0.25%. With MTP depth 3 it is severe, because there the row count is users x (depth+1) and so stays under the threshold at every low rung and pays M collectives on every step: 8 users fell from 938.4 to 398.9 tok/s (-57%) and 16 users from 974.7 to 406.4 (-58%). At 32 users with depth 3 the row count exceeds the threshold, the path is not taken, and the gap narrows to -11%.",
 "next": "The Flash-Next lane needed two row-wise switches, not one: an all-reduce switch and a second for per-row variance in a grouped RMSNorm, because that norm's mean was also reduced in a shape-dependent order. Its patch targets vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/ops/hc.py, a path Qwen3.5 does not use, so it does not transfer directly - but it says to look for a second shape-dependent reduction rather than to assume the collective is the only one. Qwen3.5's RMSNorm runs its native path here (VLLM_XPU_RMSNORM_TRITON=0) and is the obvious next suspect.",
